**[I] Q2 — Probability.** Throw one die three times; let the first, second, third results be $a,b,c$. Consider $f(x)=ax^2+bx+c$.

(1) The probability that $b=4$ and that $f(x)=0$ has two different real solutions is $\dfrac{\boxed{N}}{\boxed{OPQ}}$.

(2) Find the probability that $f(10)>453$. The number of cases $(a,b,c)$ with $f(10)>453$ is:

  when $a=4$ and $b=5$, it is $\boxed{R}$;

  when $a=4$ and $b=6$, it is $\boxed{S}$;

  when $a=5$, it is $\boxed{TU}$;

  when $a=6$, it is $\boxed{VW}$.

Hence the probability that $f(10)>453$ is $\dfrac{\boxed{X}}{\boxed{Y}}$.

**[III] — Differentiation.** Given $f(x)=x^3-3ax^2-3(2a+1)x+a+2$, answer the following.

(1) For $\boxed{G}$–$\boxed{K}$ choose from $⓪$–$⑤$ below; for the others enter numbers. Since $f'(x)=\boxed{A}(x-\boxed{B}a-\boxed{C})(x+\boxed{D})$,

  (i) when $a>\boxed{EF}$, $f(x)$ is $\boxed{G}$ at $x=-\boxed{D}$ and is $\boxed{H}$ at $x=\boxed{B}a+\boxed{C}$;

  (ii) when $a=\boxed{EF}$, $f(x)$ is always $\boxed{I}$;

  (iii) when $a<\boxed{EF}$, $f(x)$ is $\boxed{J}$ at $x=-\boxed{D}$ and is $\boxed{K}$ at $x=\boxed{B}a+\boxed{C}$.

Choices: $⓪\ \text{locally maximized}\quad ①\ \text{locally minimized}\quad ②\ \text{increasing}\quad ③\ \text{decreasing}\quad ④\ \text{maximized}\quad ⑤\ \text{minimized}$

(2) Expressing the minimum value $m$ of $f(x)$ on $-1\le x\le 1$ in terms of $a$:

  (i) when $a\ge\boxed{L}$, $m=\boxed{MN}\,a$;

  (ii) when $\boxed{OP}\le a<\boxed{L}$, $m=\boxed{QR}\left(a^3+\boxed{S}\,a^2+\boxed{T}\,a\right)$;

  (iii) when $a<\boxed{OP}$, $m=\boxed{U}\,a+\boxed{V}$.

(3) The value of $m$ in (2) is maximized at $a=\dfrac{-\boxed{W}+\sqrt{\boxed{X}}}{\boxed{Y}}$.
